<Base title="About" description="What Inside the Model is, how its interactive lessons work, and who made it.">
  <div class="wrap page">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Inside the Model teaches how AI works by letting you watch it work. Every lesson runs a small, real model in your browser, from a straight-line fit to a tiny transformer, and shows its mechanism as it happens: gradients flowing backward, attention weights forming, a policy learning from reward. You can pause it, scrub through it and change its settings.</p>

      <h2>How the lessons work</h2>
      <p>The course is a <a href="/path">fifteen-day path</a> from "what is a model?" to current research. Each lesson is one page of short steps. Each step explains one idea in plain language and pairs it with a figure you can play with. The numbers in the text are the numbers the figure shows. The math is there when you want it, folded away when you don't.</p>
      <p>The models are deliberately small, so they train in seconds on a laptop or phone. They run the same algorithms as large systems, only at a size you can watch. Where a small model behaves differently from a large one, the lesson says so.</p>
      <p>Two pages help you find your way back: the <a href="/refresher">refresher</a> collects every lesson's recap and key equation, and the <a href="/glossary">glossary</a> defines the terms.</p>

      <h2>Free and private</h2>
      <p>The site is free and needs no account. It is a set of static pages: the models run on your own device, and nothing you do here is sent to a server.</p>

